# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now.
#
# **A query is exactly one read**, and it answers with a shape or an array of them —
# detached from the database, so nothing downstream can lazily load or write through what
# it was handed.
#
# There is no Result here. Finding nothing is an **answer**, not a failure: a query that may
# find nothing answers with an array, possibly empty, and a query that asserts it found one
# answers with the shape or raises. An envelope would make every caller unwrap a value that
# was never in doubt.
#
# **A query never calls another query.** That is two reads wearing one name, the second
# invisible to whoever asked, and it is the shape an N+1 arrives in. Where a caller needs
# two reads it asks for two.
class Query
  include TypedArguments

<%- if auth -%>
  extend Permission
<%- end -%>

  # A query has no Result to carry a refusal, and wrapping one would make every caller
  # unwrap a value that was never in doubt. So it raises, like every other query failure.
  #
  # An operation implementing `anonymous_call` instead of `call` runs before anyone is
  # identified and is not checked. That is a property of the class, never of the caller.
<%- if auth -%>
  def self.call(actor: nil, **arguments)
    raise Permission::Refused, permission unless permits?(actor)

    operation = anonymous? ? new(**arguments) : new(actor: actor, **arguments)
    answer = operation.__perform__
<%- else -%>
  def self.call(**arguments)
    answer = new(**arguments).__perform__
<%- end -%>

    raise TypeError, "#{name}#call must answer with shapes, got #{answer.class}" unless shapes?(answer)

    answer
  end

  def self.shapes?(answer)
    return answer.all? { |entry| entry.is_a?(Shape) } if answer.is_a?(Array)

    answer.is_a?(Shape)
  end
  private_class_method :shapes?

  # **Reaches the private entry point with an implicit receiver**, which is the one form
  # `private` allows — `operation.call` raises, and no amount of inheritance changes that,
  # because Ruby's `private` means "no explicit receiver" rather than "inside the hierarchy".
  #
  # `send` would also work and is banned: it steps over `private`, routes around the call
  # graph, and names a method nobody can grep for. A base class that writes it cannot ask an
  # application not to.
  #
  # Public, and unreachable all the same: `new` is private, so nobody outside can hold an
  # operation to call this on.
  # `public` on purpose: this sits below the base class's own `private`, and it is called
  # with an explicit receiver from the class method above. Harmless — `new` is private, so
  # nobody outside can hold an operation to call it on.
  public def __perform__
<%- if auth -%>
    self.class.anonymous? ? anonymous_call : call
<%- else -%>
    call
<%- end -%>
  end

  # **The only way in is `call`.** A caller cannot build an operation, so it cannot reach one
  # that skipped the permission check, the transaction or the return-type assertion.
  private_class_method :new
end
